# You Might Not Need an Effect

Use Effects only to **synchronize with external systems** (browser APIs, network, third-party widgets). Avoid Effects for transforming data, handling user events, or mirroring props/state. Dependency arrays hide coupling; effect chains (A sets state → B runs) are time-based and hard to trace—prefer declarative, event-driven logic.

**References**: [react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect](https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect) · [Factory: Why we banned useEffect](https://x.com/alvinsng/status/2033969062834045089)

## Do not use Effects for

- **Derived state** — Compute during render instead of storing in state and syncing in an Effect.
- **Filtering/transforming lists** — Calculate during render; use `useMemo` only if the calculation is expensive.
- **Resetting state when a prop changes** — Prefer resetting via a `key` (e.g. `key={userId}`) so React remounts and clears state, or adjust state during render when appropriate.
- **User events** — Handle in event handlers (e.g. submit → POST in `handleSubmit`, not in an Effect watching form state).
- **Data fetching** — Use `@tanstack/react-query` (already in web) for caching, cancellation, and race-condition handling instead of `fetch` + `setState` in an Effect.
- **Chains of state updates** — Compute what you can during render and update the rest in the event handler that caused the change.
- **Notifying parent of child state** — Call the parent callback from the same event handler that updates local state, or lift state up.

**Smell tests**: State used as a flag so an effect can “do the real action” → use the event handler. Effect that only resets local state when an ID/prop changes → use `key` and remount.

## Mount-only sync: use a named hook

For the rare case of “run once on mount, cleanup on unmount” (DOM focus, third-party widget, browser API), use the named hook in `@/hooks/use-mount-effect` so intent is explicit and ad-hoc `useEffect(fn, [])` doesn’t spread:

```tsx
import { useMountEffect } from "@/hooks/use-mount-effect";
```

Prefer **conditional mounting** over guarding inside an effect: don’t mount the child until preconditions are met, then the child can safely use the mount-only hook (e.g. `if (isLoading) return <Loading />; return <VideoPlayer />` with `useMountEffect(() => playVideo())` inside `VideoPlayer`).

## Do use Effects for (or prefer alternatives)

- **Mount-only external sync** — Use `useMountEffect` (or equivalent) for DOM integration, third-party widgets, browser API subscriptions.
- Subscribing to external stores (prefer `useSyncExternalStore` when applicable).
- Fetching that must stay in sync with props only when a library isn’t feasible (with cleanup to avoid race conditions).
- Syncing with non-React systems (e.g. jQuery widget, `navigator.onLine`).

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ Redundant state + Effect
const [fullName, setFullName] = useState("");
useEffect(() => setFullName(firstName + " " + lastName), [firstName, lastName]);

// ✅ Derive during render
const fullName = firstName + " " + lastName;
```

```tsx
// ❌ Event logic in Effect
useEffect(() => {
  if (jsonToSubmit) post("/api/register", jsonToSubmit);
}, [jsonToSubmit]);

// ✅ Run in event handler
function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  post("/api/register", { firstName, lastName });
}
```

```tsx
// ❌ Resetting child state when prop changes via Effect
useEffect(() => setComment(""), [userId]);

// ✅ Reset by key so React remounts
<Profile userId={userId} key={userId} />;
```
